WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.722
Users do not always ask self-contained questions.

00:00:03.722 --> 00:00:07.107
Imagine a conversation about payment plans.

00:00:07.107 --> 00:00:11.062
The user then asks, 'Can I still do it late?'

00:00:11.062 --> 00:00:18.512
That short sentence is difficult for retrieval because it does not contain the subject of the conversation.

00:00:18.512 --> 00:00:24.271
Query rewriting creates a clearer search query while preserving the user's intent.

00:00:24.861 --> 00:00:28.813
A simple version might prepend the known conversation topic.

00:00:28.813 --> 00:00:35.963
A more advanced version might ask a language model to rewrite the question into a standalone retrieval query.

00:00:35.963 --> 00:00:38.783
The rewritten query is for search.

00:00:38.783 --> 00:00:46.398
It does not have to replace the user's original wording in the final response.

00:00:46.398 --> 00:00:49.718
This distinction is important. A good rewrite clarifies context.

00:00:49.718 --> 00:00:53.088
A bad rewrite invents assumptions.

00:00:53.088 --> 00:00:59.733
When you build conversational RAG, evaluate whether the rewrite helps retrieval without changing what the user actually

00:00:59.733 --> 00:01:01.186
meant.
